      "title": "Separate the business losses before choosing the arrangement",
      "narration": "David and a business partner review what happens if one of them dies. If the company needs funds to replace the lost services of an essential worker, key-person coverage can address that business loss. The business generally owns the policy and receives the benefit under that arrangement. A buy-sell agreement addresses a different problem: how an ownership interest will be purchased after a specified event. Life insurance can fund the purchase obligation, but the agreement establishes the transaction. In a cross-purchase design, the owners typically insure one another; in an entity-purchase design, the business generally owns the policies. Do not assume a key-person payment automatically transfers the deceased owner's shares.",
