      "title": "Separate personal-annuity withdrawals from retirement-account rules",
      "narration": "Now consider David's separate personally owned nonqualified annuity issued in 2020. It has a ninety thousand dollar value and sixty thousand dollars of adjusted investment, with no earlier withdrawals, debt or other adjustments. Before annuitization, he takes an ordinary twenty thousand dollar partial withdrawal. The thirty thousand dollars of gain is reached first, so the full twenty thousand withdrawn is taxable income under these facts. David is sixty-four, so the ordinary age-based ten percent additional tax does not apply, but the income tax remains. This is different from a properly qualifying exchange of the annuity into another annuity. That exchange can defer current gain recognition while carrying forward the relevant investment, rather than creating fresh after-tax basis at current value.",
